export const AUDIO_SCRIPTS: Readonly<Record<string, AudioScript>> = {
  "BPC-01": { en: "What blood pressure is, and why it matters.\n\nYour heart pumps blood around your body through tubes called blood vessels. Blood pressure is how hard the blood pushes on the walls of those tubes.\n\nA reading has two numbers. The top number is the push each time your heart squeezes. The bottom number is the push between beats, when your heart rests. Both matter.\n\nWhen the push stays too high for months and years, it slowly harms the walls of your blood vessels. The harm is quiet. You may feel nothing at all. Over time it can strain your heart, your kidneys, your eyes and the blood vessels in your brain.\n\nThe good news is that blood pressure can be brought down and kept steady. Taking readings at home, taking your medicines as agreed, eating a little less salt and staying active all help. You do not need to be perfect. Small steady steps count.\n\nThis course is short. Each lesson takes a few minutes and ends with one small thing to try. You can listen or read, and you can come back to any lesson at any time.\n\nYour care team is here for you. If anything in this course does not match what they have told you, follow your care team and ask them.\n\nTry this today. Find out where your blood pressure cuff is kept, or ask your care team how to get a reading near you." },
  "BPC-02": { en: "How to measure your blood pressure correctly at home.\n\nA reading is only useful if it is taken well. The same person can get very different numbers just from how they sit. These steps make your reading fair.\n\nFirst, rest. Sit quietly for five minutes before you start. Do not talk and do not scroll your phone. Do not take a reading straight after a walk, a meal, a cigarette or a cup of tea or coffee. Go to the toilet first if you need to.\n\nSit with your back supported and your feet flat on the floor. Do not cross your legs. Rest your arm on a table so the cuff is at the same level as your heart.\n\nUse the right cuff. Put it on bare skin, not over a sleeve. If the cuff is too small, the number reads too high. If it is too big, the number can read too low. If your cuff does not fit your arm well, tell your care team. A borrowed cuff may not fit you.\n\nTake two readings, about one minute apart, with the same arm. Write down both, with the time and the date. If your phone app lets you log them, do that. Do not choose the one you like better. Both are true readings.\n\nTry to measure at the same times each day, the way your care team asked. If you take a reading at a pharmacy or clinic, write down where you took it.\n\nStay calm if one reading looks odd. One reading is not the whole story. If you feel very unwell, do not keep measuring. Use the emergency guidance in the app.\n\nTry this today. Take two readings today, one minute apart, after five minutes of sitting quietly. Write down both." },
  "BPC-03": { en: "Understanding your numbers and your target.\n\nBlood pressure readings are grouped as normal, raised and high. Which group a number falls in matters, but the group is not the whole picture.\n\nYour own target is set by your care team. It depends on your age, your health and other conditions you may have. Your target may be different from someone else's, even in your own family. When your care team gives you a target, use that one, not a number you heard from a friend or read online.\n\nOne reading is not the whole story. Blood pressure goes up and down through the day. It rises when you hurry, worry, sleep badly or feel pain. It falls when you rest. That is why you take two readings and why you keep a log.\n\nWhat counts is the pattern. Are most of your readings close to your target over the week? Is the pattern moving the right way over the months? The trend in your log tells your care team much more than any single number.\n\nDo not panic about one high reading, and do not relax about one good reading. If you get a reading that your care plan tells you to act on, do what the plan says. If you are not sure what to do with a number, ask your care team.\n\nThe app shows your readings over time so you can see the pattern. You do not need to do the sums yourself.\n\nTry this today. Open your reading history in the app and look at the last week as a pattern, not as single numbers." },
  "BPC-04": { en: "Why you can feel fine with high blood pressure.\n\nMany people with high blood pressure feel completely well. Some people think they would feel it if their pressure were high. Most of the time, they would not.\n\nA headache, a hot head or a dizzy feeling can happen for many reasons, and they do not reliably tell you your pressure. Feeling fine does not mean your pressure is fine. Only a reading can tell you.\n\nThis is why blood pressure is so often missed. The harm builds quietly while you carry on with your life. A person can feel well for years and then have a stroke or heart problem with little warning.\n\nIt is also why people stop their tablets. They feel well, they think the problem has gone, and they stop. But feeling well is often a sign that the tablets are working. When tablets stop, the pressure usually climbs back, and you may not feel that either.\n\nPlease do not stop or change your tablets because you feel fine. If you want to stop, or you are worried about your tablets, talk to your care team first. Tell them the reason. They can often find a better way, such as a different tablet or a different time of day.\n\nKeep taking your readings even when you feel well. A good reading while on your tablets is the result you are looking for. It is not a reason to stop.\n\nTry this today. If you have ever skipped tablets because you felt fine, tell your care team in a message today. There is no blame." },
  "BPC-05": { en: "Your medicines: how they work and why every day.\n\nBlood pressure tablets do not fix the problem for good. They hold your pressure down while you take them. When you stop, the pressure usually rises again. That is why most people take them every day.\n\nDifferent tablets work in different ways. Some relax the blood vessels so the blood pushes less. Some help your body pass extra water and salt. Some slow the heart a little. Many people take two or three kinds together, each doing a different job. This is common and it is not a sign that you are very ill.\n\nTake your tablets at the same time every day, the way your care team told you. Link it to something you already do, like brushing your teeth or breakfast. A pill box for the week can help. The app can remind you.\n\nIf you miss a tablet, follow the instructions your care team gave you for a missed dose. If you are not sure, ask them. Do not take extra tablets to make up for a missed one unless your care team has told you to.\n\nNever run out. Ask for a refill before your tablets finish. The app can show you when you are running low. If money or distance makes it hard to get your tablets, tell your care team. They would rather know.\n\nNever share tablets and never use someone else's. Tell your care team about every medicine you take, including those from a chemist, from a shop and from traditional healers.\n\nTry this today. Choose one daily moment to link your tablets to, and set a reminder in the app for it." },
  "BPC-06": { en: "Side effects: what is common, and when to tell your care team.\n\nLike any medicine, blood pressure tablets can cause side effects. Many people have none. Some have mild ones that fade after a few weeks.\n\nSome common ones are swollen ankles, a dry cough that will not go away, and feeling tired. Some people feel dizzy when they stand up quickly, or need the toilet more often. Not every tablet causes every one of these.\n\nIf you feel dizzy when you stand, get up slowly and sit for a moment first. Drink water through the day unless you have been told to limit fluids.\n\nTell your care team about any side effect that bothers you, even a small one. There is often an easy fix, such as a different tablet or a different time of day. Use the message option in the app.\n\nPlease never stop a tablet on your own because of a side effect. Stopping suddenly can let your pressure rise. Speak to your care team first and they will guide you.\n\nSome effects need quick help. Swelling of your face, lips or tongue is one. Trouble breathing, a rash that spreads quickly, or fainting are others. Treat these as an emergency and use the emergency guidance in the app.\n\nTelling your care team about side effects is a normal part of treatment. It helps them choose the right tablets for you.\n\nTry this today. If a side effect has been bothering you, send your care team a short message today describing it." },
  "BPC-07": { en: "Salt: where it hides in Nigerian food.\n\nToo much salt makes your body hold on to water, and that makes blood pressure rise. Most of the salt we eat does not come from the salt we add at the table. It comes from what is already in the food.\n\nLook at the places salt hides. Stock cubes and seasoning powders are very salty, and many of us use two or three in one pot. Instant noodles come with a salty flavour sachet. Processed and smoked meat, salted fish, sausages and canned food carry a lot. Suya spice and many ready mixes are salty. Bread can add up too, because we eat it often.\n\nYou do not have to give up the food you love. Cut down step by step, so your taste has time to adjust.\n\nTry these small changes. Use one stock cube instead of two, then half a cube, then see if you need it at all. Add flavour with fresh pepper, onions, garlic, ginger, tomatoes and herbs such as scent leaf and thyme. Taste your food before you add salt. Cook fresh fish or meat more often than salted. Take the salt shaker off the table.\n\nWhen you buy packaged food, check the label and choose the one with less salt. Soup and stew can be made rich without much salt.\n\nYour taste changes in a few weeks. Food that tasted bland at first will start to taste right, and very salty food will start to taste too strong.\n\nIf you have kidney disease or heart failure, or your care team gave you advice about salt or fluids, follow that advice.\n\nTry this today. At your next meal, use one fewer stock cube than usual and add fresh pepper or onion for flavour." },
  "BPC-08": { en: "Eating for healthy blood pressure.\n\nYou do not need an expensive diet to help your blood pressure. Everyday Nigerian food can work well when you change the balance on the plate.\n\nFill about half your plate with vegetables, such as ugu, spinach, okra, garden egg and cabbage. Add fruit through the day, like oranges, pawpaw, banana and watermelon. These give your body more of the things that help it handle salt.\n\nTry swaps that still taste good. Use fresh pepper, onions, tomatoes and herbs instead of extra seasoning cubes. Make soup with more vegetables and a little less meat. Boil or roast plantain and yam instead of frying them. Reach for a piece of fruit instead of a sweet snack.\n\nChoose beans and other pulses more often. Beans, moi moi and akara made with less salt are a good source of protein and fibre. Fish and eggs are good too. Choose fresh over smoked or salted when you can.\n\nWatch the size of the heap of rice, yam, swallow or bread. These foods are fine, but a mountain of any of them is too much. A fist-sized portion of swallow or rice is a good start, with more vegetables beside it.\n\nUse less oil and less sugar. Fry less and boil, grill or steam more. Choose water over sugary drinks.\n\nMake changes one at a time. Pick a single swap this week and keep it. A change you keep is worth more than a perfect plan you drop.\n\nIf you have kidney disease, or your care team gave you a special diet, check with them before you change what you eat. Some foods that suit most people need care in kidney disease.\n\nTry this today. At one meal today, make vegetables fill about half your plate." },
  "BPC-09": { en: "Moving more without a gym.\n\nMoving your body helps your heart and your blood vessels, and it can help bring your blood pressure down. You do not need a gym, special clothes or any money.\n\nA good goal for most adults is about thirty minutes of moderate activity on most days. Moderate means your heart beats faster and you breathe harder, but you can still talk. You can split it up. Ten minutes three times a day counts just as well.\n\nWalking is the easiest place to start. Walk to the shop, get off the bus a stop early, or walk around the compound while you talk on the phone. Dancing counts. So does sweeping, fetching water, gardening and washing clothes by hand.\n\nIf you have not been active for a while, start slowly. Five or ten minutes is a fine start. Add a few minutes each week. Warm up with a slow walk first and cool down at the end.\n\nChoose cooler times of day, drink water, and wear comfortable shoes.\n\nStop and rest if you feel dizzy, very breathless or unwell. If you have chest pain or pressure, severe breathlessness or fainting, stop and use the emergency guidance in the app.\n\nIf you have a heart condition or any health problem that worries you, ask your care team how much activity is right for you before you start.\n\nTry this today. Take a ten-minute walk today, at a pace where you can still talk." },
  "BPC-10": { en: "Alcohol, smoking and blood pressure.\n\nThis lesson is about alcohol and tobacco. It is not about blame. Many people find these hard to change, and wanting help is normal.\n\nAlcohol raises blood pressure, and the effect builds up when you drink often. It can also make some tablets work less well and add extra calories. If you drink, drinking less and less often is better for your pressure. If you do not drink, there is no need to start.\n\nEach cigarette makes your blood pressure and heart rate jump for a short while. Smoking also harms the walls of your blood vessels directly. Together with high blood pressure, smoking raises the chance of a heart attack or stroke far more than either one alone. Smokeless tobacco such as snuff and chewing tobacco also raises blood pressure. Switching to it is not a safe answer.\n\nQuitting is hard and most people need more than one try. Trying again is not failing. It is how most people succeed.\n\nA practical first step is to cut down a little. Skip one drink or one cigarette each day. Notice when you most want one, such as after meals, with friends or when stressed. Plan something else for that moment, like a short walk or a glass of water.\n\nTell your care team if you drink or use tobacco. They will not judge you. They can give you real support and plan with you.\n\nTry this today. Choose one small cut for this week, such as one drink or one cigarette less each day, and write it down." },
  "BPC-11": { en: "Stress, sleep and blood pressure.\n\nWhen you are stressed, your body releases hormones that make your heart beat faster and your blood vessels tighten. That raises your blood pressure for a while. This is useful in a real emergency. It is not helpful when the stress is traffic, money worries or a long day running on a loop.\n\nPoor sleep does something similar. People who sleep badly night after night often have higher blood pressure. If your partner says you snore loudly or stop breathing for a moment while you sleep, tell your care team. This can be treated.\n\nYou cannot remove all stress, and you do not need to. What helps is having a reliable way to come back down. Some people pray or have quiet time. Some walk, talk to a friend or listen to calm music. Slow breathing for a few minutes is something you can do anywhere.\n\nThe app has a short guided breathing exercise. It is a calm moment, and many people find it helpful. It does not replace your tablets or your readings. Keep taking your medicines and measuring as agreed with your care team.\n\nFor better sleep, try to go to bed and wake at about the same time each day. Keep the room dark and quiet. Put the phone away for the last half hour. Avoid tea, coffee and heavy meals late in the evening.\n\nIf stress, low mood or poor sleep is affecting you most days, tell your care team. It is a health matter and they can help.\n\nTry this today. Try the three-minute breathing exercise in the app once today, sitting down." },
  "BPC-12": { en: "Herbal remedies and blood pressure.\n\nMany families trust herbal remedies, and that trust comes from real experience of care. This lesson is not about blaming anyone. It is about keeping you safe.\n\nHerbal drinks and mixtures are not a replacement for your blood pressure tablets. For most of them, nobody has shown that they control blood pressure safely, or in what amount. Stopping your tablets and relying on a herbal mixture can let your pressure rise without you feeling it.\n\nSome herbal products can also interact with your medicines. A product can make a tablet work too strongly or too weakly, or harm your kidneys or liver. A natural product is not automatically safe. The strength can also differ from one bottle or one seller to the next, and the label may not tell you what is inside.\n\nPlease tell your care team about everything you take, including herbal drinks, powders, bitter leaf, garlic or ginger preparations, bottled mixtures and supplements. They will not scold you. They need the full picture so that your treatment is safe.\n\nSome foods and spices are part of a healthy diet. Using garlic or ginger in your cooking is fine. The concern is concentrated mixtures taken as a treatment, or taken in place of tablets.\n\nBefore you start any new product, check with your care team first. If you have already stopped your tablets to use a herbal product, please tell your care team so that they can help you restart safely.\n\nTry this today. Write down everything you take apart from your tablets and share the list with your care team in a message." },
  "BPC-13": { en: "Warning signs that need urgent care.\n\nMost of the time, blood pressure care is steady and calm. Sometimes a warning sign means you need help right away.\n\nGet emergency help now if you have any of these. A very bad headache that is not like your usual one. Chest pain or a heavy feeling in the chest. Trouble breathing. Weakness or numbness in the face, an arm or a leg. Trouble speaking, or confusion. Sudden problems with your sight.\n\nCall one one two or go to the nearest hospital emergency department. If you can, ask someone to come with you or to drive you. Do not drive yourself if you feel faint, confused or weak.\n\nDo not wait to see if it passes. Do not take another reading to decide. Do not try a home or herbal remedy first. These signs can be a stroke or a heart emergency, and every minute counts.\n\nThe app has emergency guidance that works even without data. You can open it at any time from the emergency button. It shows what to do and who to call.\n\nTell the people at home about these signs ahead of time. If you cannot speak for yourself, they will know what to do.\n\nIf you are not sure whether something is an emergency, treat it as one and get help. Your care team would always prefer that you came early.\n\nTry this today. Find the emergency button in the app today and tell one person at home what these warning signs are." },
  "BPC-14": { en: "Staying in control for life.\n\nWell done for reaching the end of this course. Taking care of your blood pressure is a long road, and you are already on it.\n\nIn the first few months, you and your care team find what works for you. That may mean a change of tablets or a change in timing. This is normal. Keep sending your readings so they can see the pattern.\n\nAfter that, many people settle into a steady routine. Keep taking your readings, take your tablets every day, eat less salt, stay active, and keep up the habits that you built.\n\nBlood pressure can harm the kidneys, the heart and the eyes without any feeling, so keep to your regular checks. Your care team will plan yearly checks for these. Please do not skip them because you feel well.\n\nExpect good days and harder days. A busy week, a funeral, a wedding, a festival or a trip can disturb your routine. When that happens, restart the next day. You have not failed.\n\nAsk for help early. Tell your care team when tablets are running low, when a side effect bothers you or when life gets difficult. Talk to your family so that they can support you.\n\nKeep this course. You can come back to any lesson whenever you want a reminder.\n\nTry this today. Write down one habit from this course that you will keep, and tell someone at home what it is." },
  "BRE-01": { en: "Three minute calm. Sit comfortably, with your back supported and your feet on the floor. Let your shoulders drop. There is nothing to achieve here. If you feel dizzy, or anything feels wrong, stop and breathe normally. Breathe gently through your nose. We will breathe in for four, and out for six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nBreathe in, two, three, four. Breathe out, two, three, four, five, six.\n\nGently let your breathing return to normal. Notice how your body feels. When you are ready, open your eyes. Keep taking your medicines and measuring your blood pressure as agreed with your care team." },
  "CON-001": { en: "Before we begin: your clinician would like to use an AI assistant to help write the notes from this consultation. It drafts a note that the clinician checks and signs, and nothing is saved to your record until they do. You can say no, and the clinician will write the notes themselves. Is that all right with you?" },
  "CON-002": { en: "Your clinician will join you shortly. Please stay on this screen." },
  "CON-003": { en: "The connection is weak, so we are switching to audio only to keep the call going." },
  "CON-004": { en: "We are moving this consultation to a normal phone call. Please answer when your phone rings." },
  "CON-005": { en: "Your consultation has ended. Your summary, care plan and any prescription will appear in My Health shortly." },
  "CON-006": { en: "Your question has been sent to the care team. You will get a reply within the time shown on screen." },
  "CON-007": { en: "Your prescription has been sent to the pharmacy you chose. You can collect it and pay there." },
  "CON-008": { en: "Your referral letter is ready in My Health. Take it with you, or show it on your phone." },
  "CON-009": { en: "Welcome to your care pack. For the next three months, our care team will support you. Log your readings as planned, take your medicines, and we will review your progress and contact you if anything needs attention." },
  "CON-010": { en: "Your care pack ends in seven days. It will not renew by itself. If you would like to continue, you can buy another from Care." },
  "CON-011": { en: "A clinician from your care team will call you soon. Please keep your phone nearby." },
  "CON-012": { en: "We have booked a follow-up for you. You can see the time in Care." },
  "EMG-001": { en: "Your reading and how you feel mean you need care now. Go to the nearest hospital, or ask someone to take you. Do not drive yourself. Tell them your blood pressure and your symptoms." },
  "EMG-001L": { en: "Sit or lie down now, and raise your legs if you can. Go to the nearest hospital, or ask someone to take you. Stand up slowly and do not drive yourself." },
  "EMG-002": { en: "These may be signs of a stroke. Check the face: has it dropped on one side? Check the arms: can both be raised? Check the speech: is it slurred or strange? If you see any of these, time matters. Call one one two or go to the nearest hospital emergency department now. Note the time the signs started." },
  "EMG-003": { en: "Chest pain or pressure that lasts more than a few minutes, especially with sweating, breathlessness or pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back, can be a heart attack. Call one one two or go to the nearest hospital emergency department now. Sit down and rest while you wait. Do not drive yourself." },
  "EMG-004": { en: "Your blood sugar is low. If you can swallow safely, take something sugary now, such as half a bottle of a regular soft drink or three teaspoons of sugar in water. Check your sugar again in fifteen minutes. If it is still low, take sugar again. If you become drowsy or confused, someone should call one one two." },
  "EMG-005": { en: "Your blood sugar is very high. If you are vomiting, very thirsty, breathing fast, drowsy or confused, go to the nearest hospital emergency department now or call one one two. Keep drinking water if you can." },
  "EMG-006": { en: "If someone is having a seizure, keep them safe. Move hard objects away and put something soft under their head. Do not hold them down and do not put anything in their mouth. When the shaking stops, turn them onto their side. Call one one two if the seizure lasts more than five minutes, if it is their first seizure, or if they are hurt or pregnant." },
  "EMG-007": { en: "If you are struggling to breathe, cannot finish a sentence, or your lips are turning grey or blue, call one one two or go to the nearest hospital emergency department now. If you have a reliever inhaler, use it as your clinician showed you while you get help." },
  "EMG-008": { en: "In pregnancy, go to hospital straight away if you have bleeding from the vagina, a severe headache or blurred vision, fits, severe pain in your belly, fever, your waters breaking early, or your baby moving less than usual. Do not wait. Call one one two or go now." },
  "EMG-009": { en: "Take your child to hospital straight away if they cannot drink or breastfeed, are vomiting everything, have had a fit, are very sleepy or hard to wake, are breathing fast or with difficulty, or are a young baby with a fever. Do not wait." },
  "EMG-010": { en: "Swelling of the face, lips or tongue, difficulty breathing or a sudden rash with feeling faint can be a serious allergic reaction. Call one one two or go to the nearest hospital emergency department now. If you have an adrenaline pen, use it." },
  "EMG-011": { en: "Thank you for telling us. You do not have to face this alone. A member of our care team is being asked to contact you now. If you are in immediate danger, call one one two or go to the nearest hospital." },
  "EMG-012": { en: "We have also let your chosen Care Circle contact know, so someone can be with you." },
  "EMG-013": { en: "You are offline. This guidance is saved on your phone and will still work." },
  "HLP-001": { en: "This screen shows what to do today. Finish each task and it will be ticked off." },
  "HLP-002": { en: "Use this to record a reading or how you feel. It takes a few seconds, and it saves even without internet." },
  "HLP-003": { en: "Sit quietly for five minutes with your back supported and your arm resting at the level of your heart. Then take your reading and type in both numbers." },
  "HLP-004": { en: "Type in your reading and choose when you took it: before food, after food, or at bedtime. The timing helps us understand the number." },
  "HLP-005": { en: "Weigh yourself at the same time each day, ideally in the morning, for the most useful trend." },
  "HLP-006": { en: "Choose the face that best matches how you feel. Your answers are private." },
  "HLP-007": { en: "Record anything you notice, such as headache or dizziness. Your care team can see the pattern over time." },
  "HLP-008": { en: "This chart shows your readings over time. The shaded band is your target. Readings inside the band are where we want them to be." },
  "HLP-009": { en: "Your Health Passport keeps all your health information in one place. You can share parts of it with any hospital or clinic you choose." },
  "HLP-010": { en: "Your emergency card shows your blood group, genotype, allergies, conditions, medicines and emergency contacts. It works without internet and can be shown from your lock screen." },
  "HLP-011": { en: "Create a link or code to share chosen records with a clinic. It expires automatically, and you can stop it at any time." },
  "HLP-012": { en: "Your results appear here with a plain explanation. Results outside the normal range are checked by a clinician before you see them." },
  "HLP-013": { en: "This shows the checks recommended for your age and health, and when each one is due." },
  "HLP-014": { en: "Once a year we bring your results and readings together into one report: what has changed, what is on target, and your priorities for the year ahead." },
  "HLP-015": { en: "These are your medicines and when to take them. Tap a medicine to see how to take it, or to mark a dose as taken." },
  "HLP-016": { en: "This shows how many of your doses you took this week. Taking medicines every day is one of the most powerful things you can do for your health." },
  "HLP-017": { en: "Compare prices at partner pharmacies, then choose where to collect your medicine. You pay the pharmacy when you collect." },
  "HLP-018": { en: "Answer a few questions about how you feel. We will suggest what might be causing it, how urgent it is, and what to do next. This is not a diagnosis." },
  "HLP-019": { en: "Choose a video, audio or written consultation. You will see the price before you pay." },
  "HLP-020": { en: "Write your question and add a photo if it helps. A clinician will reply within the time shown." },
  "HLP-021": { en: "Find hospitals, clinics, labs and pharmacies near you, with their opening hours and services." },
  "HLP-022": { en: "Choose a test and a collection point near you. You pay the lab price shown at checkout." },
  "HLP-023": { en: "A care pack gives you three months of support from our care team for your condition. It does not renew by itself." },
  "HLP-024": { en: "Check what you are paying for, then pay by card, bank transfer or USSD. You will get a receipt in the app." },
  "HLP-025": { en: "You can pay for a care pack, a test or a consultation for someone in your Care Circle, from anywhere in the world." },
  "HLP-026": { en: "Your Care Circle is the family and friends you trust to support you. Choose what each person can see, and change it at any time." },
  "HLP-027": { en: "Keep your children's health records, growth and vaccines here, all under your account." },
  "HLP-028": { en: "This shows vaccines given and vaccines due. We will remind you before each one is due." },
  "HLP-029": { en: "Short lessons and audio about health, checked by clinicians. Tap the play button to listen." },
  "HLP-030": { en: "Guided meditations, sleep audio and breathing exercises. Download them on Wi-Fi to listen without using data." },
  "HLP-031": { en: "Short workouts you can do at home with no equipment. Choose your level and start." },
  "HLP-032": { en: "Choose what you ate and how much, using everyday portions like a plate, a wrap or a spoon." },
  "HLP-033": { en: "This shows your calories, salt, sugar and other nutrients for today against your targets." },
  "HLP-034": { en: "You earn Health Points for looking after your health every day. Use them for discounts on tests and consultations." },
  "HLP-035": { en: "Connect a blood pressure monitor, glucometer, scale or watch to record readings automatically. Typing readings in always works too." },
  "HLP-036": { en: "Choose who can see each type of information, and for what purpose. You can change these at any time." },
  "HLP-037": { en: "Discreet mode hides condition names from notifications and can lock the app with a PIN." },
  "HLP-038": { en: "Low-data mode saves your data by loading text first. Audio and video play only when you ask." },
  "HLP-039": { en: "On a phone without internet, dial the Tarragon short code to log a reading, confirm a dose or ask for a call back." },
  "HLP-040": { en: "Follow your pregnancy week by week, with reminders for antenatal visits and advice on warning signs." },
  "HLP-041": { en: "Guided programmes for sleep, worry, mood and pain. Do one short session at a time." },
  "NAV-001": { en: "This is Home. At the top you will see today's tasks, such as a reading to log or a medicine to take. Tap a task to do it. Below that are your reminders and your next step. If something does not feel right, tap Check a symptom. To speak to a clinician, tap See a doctor." },
  "NAV-002": { en: "This is My Health. Everything about your health is kept here: your readings and trends, your medicines, your test results, your screening calendar and your emergency card. This is your Health Passport, and it stays with you wherever you go." },
  "NAV-003": { en: "This is Care. Here you can check a symptom, book a consultation, send a question to a clinician, book a lab test, send a prescription to your pharmacy, and find hospitals and clinics near you. Emergency guidance is always here too, even without internet." },
  "NAV-004": { en: "This is Wellbeing. Here you will find short lessons, meditations, sleep audio, breathing exercises, home workouts and food guidance. Everything here is free. Start with anything that feels right for you today." },
  "NAV-005": { en: "This is Family. Add your children, keep track of their vaccines and growth, and manage your Care Circle: the people you trust to support your health. You decide exactly what each person can see." },
  "NAV-006": { en: "This button opens your health assistant. Ask it anything about your readings, your results or your medicines, by typing or by speaking. It explains things in plain language. If something needs a clinician, it will tell you and help you get one." },
  "NAV-007": { en: "Your profile menu holds your payments and receipts, language, low-data mode, privacy settings and help. You can change your language here at any time." },
  "NUM-D01": { en: "point" },
  "NUM-P01": { en: "Your blood pressure reading is" },
  "NUM-P02": { en: "over" },
  "NUM-P03": { en: "Your blood sugar reading is" },
  "NUM-P04": { en: "milligrams per decilitre" },
  "NUM-P05": { en: "millimoles per litre" },
  "NUM-P06": { en: "Your weight is" },
  "NUM-P07": { en: "kilograms" },
  "NUM-P08": { en: "Your pulse is" },
  "NUM-P09": { en: "beats per minute" },
  "NUM-P10": { en: "Your HbA1c is" },
  "NUM-P11": { en: "percent" },
  "NUM-P12": { en: "Your average this week is" },
  "NUM-P13": { en: "That is higher than last week." },
  "NUM-P14": { en: "That is lower than last week." },
  "NUM-P15": { en: "That is about the same as last week." },
  "NUM-P16": { en: "This week you have taken" },
  "NUM-P17": { en: "out of" },
  "NUM-P18": { en: "doses." },
  "NUM-P19": { en: "You have logged for" },
  "NUM-P20": { en: "days in a row." },
  "NUM-P21": { en: "Today you have walked" },
  "NUM-P23": { en: "steps." },
  "NUM-P24": { en: "millimetres of mercury" },
  "ONB-001": { en: "Welcome to Tarragon Health. To continue in English, tap English." },
  "ONB-002": { en: "Welcome to Tarragon Health. We help you know your health, manage it every day, and get a clinician when you need one. Setting up takes about five minutes." },
  "ONB-003": { en: "Enter your phone number or your email address. You will use it to sign in." },
  "ONB-004": { en: "We have sent a six-digit code to your phone by text message. Type it in to confirm your number. This makes sure we never use a wrong number." },
  "ONB-005": { en: "We have sent a code to your email address. Type it in to confirm your email." },
  "ONB-006": { en: "Now create a password. Use at least eight characters, and do not use a password you use anywhere else." },
  "ONB-007": { en: "Would you like to open Tarragon Health with your fingerprint or face? It is quicker, and your information stays protected." },
  "ONB-008": { en: "If your employer, HMO or community group gave you a code, enter it here. If not, tap Skip." },
  "ONB-009": { en: "Your health information belongs to you. We ask your permission before we use it, and you can change your mind at any time in Settings. We never sell your information, and there are no adverts in this app." },
  "ONB-010": { en: "Here is how we look after your information. We keep your readings, results, medicines and messages so that you and your care team can look after your health. Your care team sees your record so they can help you. Your family in your Care Circle sees only what you choose to share. If your employer or HMO pays for your care, they never see your personal record. You can download your information or delete your account at any time from Settings." },
  "ONB-011": { en: "What matters most to you right now? Choose one: blood pressure, blood sugar, weight, pregnancy, your children's vaccines, a general check-up, or supporting a parent. You can add more later." },
  "ONB-012": { en: "Now a few short questions about you. There are no wrong answers, and it takes about two minutes." },
  "ONB-013": { en: "Thank you. Here is where you stand today, and your first step." },
  "ONB-014": { en: "This is your Home screen. Each day it shows the one or two most important things to do. When you are ready, tap the green button to start." },
  "ONB-015": { en: "You are setting up Tarragon Health for your parent. When you finish, we will send a code to their phone. They will enter it to confirm the account is theirs." },
  "ONB-016": { en: "Someone in your family has set up Tarragon Health for you. Enter the code we sent to your phone to confirm. The account is yours, and you decide what to share." },
  "ONB-017": { en: "You can add your children to your account, so their growth, vaccines and health records are kept safely in one place." },
  "ONB-018": { en: "Would you like someone in your family to support you? Invite them to your Care Circle. You choose exactly what they can see, and you can remove them at any time." },
  "REM-001": { en: "Good morning. It is time to check your blood pressure. Sit quietly for five minutes first." },
  "REM-002": { en: "It is time for your morning medicines." },
  "REM-003": { en: "It is time for your afternoon medicines." },
  "REM-004": { en: "It is time for your evening medicines." },
  "REM-005": { en: "It is time for your bedtime medicines." },
  "REM-006": { en: "It is time to check your blood sugar, before you eat." },
  "REM-007": { en: "It has been two hours since your meal. It is time to check your blood sugar." },
  "REM-008": { en: "It looks like you have not marked your medicine as taken. If you have taken it, tap Taken. If you missed it, tap the medicine to see what to do." },
  "REM-009": { en: "Your medicine will soon run out. Please plan to collect more from your pharmacy." },
  "REM-010": { en: "A health check is due. Open the app to book it." },
  "REM-011": { en: "Your consultation with the care team is tomorrow. Make sure your phone is charged." },
  "REM-012": { en: "Your consultation starts soon. Find a quiet place with good network." },
  "REM-013": { en: "You have a new message from your care team." },
  "REM-014": { en: "Your results are ready. Open My Health to see them." },
  "REM-015": { en: "You have logged every day this week. That is how health changes. Well done." },
  "REM-016": { en: "We have not heard from you for a few days. How are you doing? Log a reading today, even a quick one." },
  "REM-017": { en: "Take a moment for yourself today. A three-minute breathing exercise is ready for you." },
  "REM-018": { en: "It is nearly time for sleep. Put your phone down soon, and try a wind-down audio if you like." },
  "REM-019": { en: "Remember to drink some water." },
  "REM-020": { en: "Your weekly summary is ready. See how your week went." },
  "REM-021": { en: "A vaccine is due soon. Open Family to see which one and where to get it." },
  "REM-022": { en: "There is a new weekly update from someone you support." },
  "RES-001": { en: "Good news. All your results are within the normal range. Keep up what you are doing, and we will remind you when your next check is due." },
  "RES-002": { en: "Some of your results are outside the normal range. A clinician has reviewed them, and your next step is shown below." },
  "RES-003": { en: "Your results have arrived. A clinician is checking them before they are released to you. You will be notified soon." },
  "RES-004": { en: "Your blood pressure is in the healthy range. Check it again at least once a year." },
  "RES-005": { en: "Your blood pressure is raised. This often causes no symptoms, but over time it can damage the heart, brain and kidneys. Checking it at home over the next week will show whether it stays high." },
  "RES-006": { en: "Your blood pressure is high. This needs treatment to protect your heart, brain and kidneys. Our care team can help you bring it under control." },
  "RES-007": { en: "Your fasting blood sugar is normal." },
  "RES-008": { en: "Your fasting blood sugar is higher than normal, but not in the diabetes range. This is a warning sign. Changes to food, activity and weight now can stop diabetes developing." },
  "RES-009": { en: "Your fasting blood sugar is in the diabetes range. A second test is usually needed to confirm it. Our care team will guide you on the next step." },
  "RES-010": { en: "Your HbA1c, which shows your average blood sugar over the last three months, is normal." },
  "RES-011": { en: "Your HbA1c shows your average blood sugar has been higher than normal, but not in the diabetes range. Now is the best time to act." },
  "RES-012": { en: "Your HbA1c is in the diabetes range. Our care team will talk you through what this means and the next step." },
  "RES-013": { en: "Your HbA1c is above your target. Your care team will review your treatment with you." },
  "RES-014": { en: "Your HbA1c is on target. Your hard work is paying off." },
  "RES-015": { en: "Your cholesterol is in the healthy range." },
  "RES-016": { en: "Your cholesterol is raised. Together with your other results, it helps us understand your risk of heart disease and stroke. Your care team will advise you." },
  "RES-017": { en: "Your kidney function is normal." },
  "RES-018": { en: "Your kidney function is lower than expected. Avoid painkillers such as ibuprofen and diclofenac unless a clinician has advised them. Your care team will review this with you." },
  "RES-019": { en: "Your urine test shows protein. This can be an early sign of kidney strain, often from high blood pressure or diabetes. Your care team will advise on the next step." },
  "RES-020": { en: "Your blood count shows a low level of haemoglobin, which is called anaemia. This can cause tiredness. Your care team will look into the cause." },
  "RES-021": { en: "Some of your liver tests are outside the normal range. There are many possible reasons, and your care team will advise on the next step." },
  "RES-022": { en: "Your thyroid test is outside the normal range. Your care team will explain what this means for you." },
  "RES-023": { en: "Your HIV, hepatitis B and hepatitis C tests are all negative." },
  "RES-024": { en: "Your test shows you are protected against hepatitis B, so you will not need yearly hepatitis B checks." },
  "RES-025": { en: "Your risk of heart attack or stroke over the next ten years is low. Keep it that way with the habits in Wellbeing." },
  "RES-026": { en: "Your risk of heart attack or stroke over the next ten years is moderate. Small changes now can lower it." },
  "RES-027": { en: "Your risk of heart attack or stroke over the next ten years is high. Our care team can help you lower it, and it is worth acting now." },
  "RES-028": { en: "Your weight is above the healthy range for your height. Losing even a small amount can lower your blood pressure and blood sugar." },
  "RES-029": { en: "Remember, these tests are a helpful check but cannot rule out every illness. If you feel unwell, do not wait for your next check." },
  "SYM-001": { en: "Let us find out what might be going on. I will ask some questions about how you feel. Answer as best you can." },
  "SYM-002": { en: "First, a few important safety questions." },
  "SYM-003": { en: "Here are the most likely causes, based on your answers. This is not a diagnosis. Only a clinician can confirm what is wrong." },
  "SYM-004": { en: "This can usually be looked after at home. Here is what to do, and the signs that mean you should get help." },
  "SYM-005": { en: "A pharmacist can help with this. You can find one near you in Care." },
  "SYM-006": { en: "You should see a clinician within the next few days. You can book a consultation now." },
  "SYM-007": { en: "You should speak to a clinician within twenty-four hours. You can book or send a question now." },
  "SYM-008": { en: "You should be seen today. Book a consultation or go to a clinic near you today." },
  "SYM-009": { en: "This could be serious. Get emergency help now." },
  "SYM-010": { en: "Would you like a clinician to review your answers? You will see the price before you pay." },
  "SYM-011": { en: "Your answers will be shared with the clinician, so you will not need to repeat yourself." },
  "SYS-001": { en: "You are offline. You can keep logging, and everything will be sent when you are back online." },
  "SYS-002": { en: "You are back online. Your information is up to date." },
  "SYS-003": { en: "Low-data mode is on. Audio and video will play only when you tap them." },
  "SYS-004": { en: "This is a large file. We will download it when you connect to Wi-Fi." },
  "SYS-005": { en: "Payment successful. Your receipt is in your profile menu." },
  "SYS-006": { en: "The payment did not go through. You have not been charged. Please try again or choose another way to pay." },
  "SYS-007": { en: "Your refund has been processed. It may take a few days to reach your account." },
  "SYS-008": { en: "Something went wrong on our side. Please try again. If it keeps happening, contact support from Help." },
  "SYS-009": { en: "For your privacy, the app has locked. Use your fingerprint, face or PIN to open it." },
  "SYS-010": { en: "A new version of Tarragon Health is ready. Update when you are on Wi-Fi." },
  "SYS-011": { en: "Our support team is available during the hours shown. Outside those hours, leave a message and we will reply as soon as we are back. For anything urgent, use emergency guidance." },
  "TRI-001": { en: "This reading is within your target. Well done. Keep checking at the times you planned." },
  "TRI-002": { en: "This reading is higher than your target. Rest, then check again at your next time. If you feel unwell or worse, go to the nearest hospital." },
  "TRI-003": { en: "This reading is a little above your target. This is not an emergency. Rest, take your medicines as planned, and check again at your next time. If it stays high over the next few days, book a review with your care team. If you feel unwell, go to the nearest hospital." },
  "TRI-004": { en: "This reading needs attention now." },
  "TRI-005": { en: "Sit quietly for five minutes with your arm supported. Then measure once more and save it." },
  "TRI-006": { en: "That reading does not look right. Check the numbers you typed, or measure again." },
  "TRI-007": { en: "This reading is lower than your target. If you feel dizzy, faint or unwell, tap Check a symptom." },
  "TRI-008": { en: "Your reading is saved on your phone and will be sent when you are back online." },
};
